WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.979
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:08.630
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:16.029
Unit fifteen: Historical Method. Today you will make precise claims about limits with modal + adverb combinations.

4
00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:19.016
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:22.863
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:25.066
Historiography.

7
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.443
Historiography means the study of how history is written.

8
00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.071
Historiography shows how views change over time.

9
00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:36.394
Your turn. Say: Historiography.

10
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:40.606
Historiography.

11
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:42.676
Source criticism.

12
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:47.749
Source criticism means evaluating the reliability of sources.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.197
Source criticism asks who wrote a text and why.

14
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:54.645
Your turn. Say: Source criticism.

15
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.024
Source criticism.

16
00:00:59.783 --> 00:01:00.610
Bias.

17
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:04.127
Bias means a tendency to favour one view.

18
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:06.972
Every source carries some bias.

19
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:09.945
Your turn. Say: Bias.

20
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.801
Bias.

21
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.934
Periodization.

22
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.261
Periodization means dividing the past into periods.

23
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.442
Periodization can hide continuities.

24
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.482
Your turn. Say: Periodization.

25
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.578
Periodization.

26
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:32.283
Causation.

27
00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:36.258
Causation means the study of causes and effects.

28
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.326
Causation is rarely simple in history.

29
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:42.372
Your turn. Say: Causation.

30
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.467
Causation.

31
00:01:47.217 --> 00:01:48.636
Interpretation.

32
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.393
Interpretation means an explanation of meaning.

33
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:55.784
Her interpretation challenged the consensus.

34
00:01:56.383 --> 00:01:59.163
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

35
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.175
Interpretation.

36
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:04.860
Evidence.

37
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.593
Evidence means information that supports a claim.

38
00:02:09.183 --> 00:02:11.659
The evidence is limited but consistent.

39
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.821
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

40
00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:18.668
Evidence.

41
00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:20.511
Objectivity.

42
00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:24.863
Objectivity means judgement free from personal feelings.

43
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.692
Complete objectivity is an ideal, not a reality.

44
00:02:29.283 --> 00:02:32.191
Your turn. Say: Objectivity.

45
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:36.197
Objectivity.

46
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.500
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.580
Visal, can a historian ever be completely objective?

48
00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:54.930
I don't think so. A historian can rarely be completely objective, because choosing which sources to study is already an interpretation.

49
00:02:56.017 --> 00:02:57.749
Then is history just opinion?

50
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:09.351
No. Bias can never fully be removed, but it can certainly be reduced through source criticism and by comparing different perspectives.

51
00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.775
How would you apply that to the Khmer Empire?

52
00:03:13.883 --> 00:03:23.560
Temple inscriptions mostly praise kings, so they can seldom tell us about farmers. We can only partly fill that gap with archaeology.

53
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.235
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.083 --> 00:03:32.369
Grammar focus: Modal plus adverb (can never fully, can rarely).

55
00:03:32.669 --> 00:03:33.902
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.617 --> 00:03:37.417
A historian can rarely be completely objective.

57
00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:40.397
Bias can never fully be removed.

58
00:03:41.283 --> 00:03:44.653
Inscriptions can seldom tell us about ordinary farmers.

59
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:56.902
Adverbs make modal claims precise: can rarely / seldom (not often), can never fully (not completely), can only partly. Place the adverb after the modal: 'can never fully be', not 'can fully never be'.

60
00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:02.230
Be careful! Don't say: A historian can fully never be objective.

61
00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:07.176
Say: A historian can never be fully objective.

62
00:04:07.476 --> 00:04:10.149
Order: can + never + be + fully.

63
00:04:10.933 --> 00:04:13.481
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:15.779
The Historian's Craft.

65
00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:21.375
The historical method is a set of practices for turning fragmentary evidence into reliable knowledge.

66
00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:28.615
Historians can rarely observe the past directly; instead, they reconstruct it through sources that are always partial.

67
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.315
Source criticism asks who produced a document, for what purpose and under what constraints.

68
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:42.026
Because selection and interpretation are unavoidable, complete objectivity can never fully be achieved.

69
00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:49.456
Rigorous method, transparency and debate nevertheless allow historians to reach conclusions that can be trusted.

70
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:54.603
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.333
Bias can blank be eliminated.

72
00:04:57.633 --> 00:05:01.763
A, never fully. B, fully never. C, never fully to.

73
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.625
The answer is A: never fully.

74
00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:11.491
A historian can blank observe the past directly.

75
00:05:11.791 --> 00:05:15.061
A, rarely. B, rare. C, rarely to.

76
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.356
The answer is A: rarely.

77
00:05:21.517 --> 00:05:24.457
Evaluating the reliability of sources is blank.

78
00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:29.420
A, periodization. B, source criticism. C, causation.

79
00:05:32.967 --> 00:05:35.454
The answer is B: source criticism.

80
00:05:36.617 --> 00:05:39.313
Judgement free from personal feelings is blank.

81
00:05:39.613 --> 00:05:43.533
A, evidence. B, bias. C, objectivity.

82
00:05:47.067 --> 00:05:49.438
The answer is C: objectivity.

83
00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:54.986
According to the reading, what allows historians to reach trusted conclusions?

84
00:05:55.286 --> 00:06:01.529
A, complete objectivity. B, rigorous method, transparency and debate. C, a single source.

85
00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:09.052
The answer is B: rigorous method, transparency and debate.

86
00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:13.418
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.283 --> 00:06:18.317
Can a historian ever be completely objective? Why or why not?

88
00:06:35.117 --> 00:06:36.594
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.894 --> 00:07:10.982
A historian can never be completely objective, because every decision about which sources to use and which questions to ask involves judgement. Historians are also influenced by their own culture and time. For example, colonial historians often described Khmer history through European assumptions. Nevertheless, this does not make history mere opinion, since claims can still be tested against evidence. I believe the best historians admit their limits openly and invite others to challenge their conclusions.

90
00:07:11.282 --> 00:07:12.980
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:13.883 --> 00:07:19.728
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal plus adverb (can never fully, can rarely).

92
00:07:20.028 --> 00:07:25.305
Today's value is intellectual humility. Admit what you do not know, and stay ready to learn.

93
00:07:25.605 --> 00:07:28.419
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:31.917
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

